{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "100",
    "case_summary": "A 14-month-old female presented with significant developmental delay, cardiac anomalies, preauricular tags, dysmorphic features, polysplenia, extrahepatic biliary atresia, intestinal malrotation, and hearing loss in the right ear. The patient experienced thrombocytopenia following a liver transplant. Bone marrow examination revealed complete replacement with chronic lymphocytic leukemia and a marked reduction in erythroid precursors, consistent with pure red cell aplasia. After undergoing a bone marrow transplant, the patient remained in remission. The parents exhibited no phenotypic abnormalities.\n\nA de novo sSMC was present in all studied cells of this case. The NOR-positive sSMC was initially characterized as a derivative of chromosome 13 or 21 by application of the corresponding commercially available centromeric probes for #13/21 (D13/21Z1) and #15 (D15Z1). As a probe specific for the short arms of all acrocentric chromosomes (midi54) gave two signals on the sSMC also the probe for the centromeric regions 14 or 22 (D14/22Z1) was applied and gave one signal on the sSMC, as well. The two centromere-near probes RP11-172D7 and RP11-81B3 in 22q11.21 gave two co-localized signals, each, while the probe RP11-1058B20 in 22q11.22 was absent on the sSMC. In summary, a dic(13 or 21;22)(13 or 21pter->13 or21q11::22q11.1~11.2->22q11.21~11.22::22q11.21~11.22->22pter) was characterized.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
